# PAIRED UPSTREAM-VS-FORK SCALING, RE-RUN ON THE CURRENT DEFAULT ROUTE.
#
# WHY THIS REPLACES dd_upstream_vs_v3.tsv. That campaign measured the fork at v7.1.3-omp.3, where
# dE3's DEFAULT route was dense. The route promotion changed dE3's default to sparse, so the older
# table showed a route the fork no longer takes -- understating its own default on dE3 by 5.1x.
# The point of an upstream comparison is "what each build does by default", so it had to be redone
# rather than relabelled. dE4 is unaffected either way: it routes sparse under both policies.
#
# PROVENANCE (captured by the run)
#   fork commit      c80ca77670b3dafd5f372d23cf54440e6d9f6963 (main), clean tree
#   fork binary      2ec45737f9977acb7165d264d16fe13d721783ce79a53d51c3c59e8eeb2fcf84
#   upstream commit  6eaad8d9abff929bf8abc55ea166cbb2b09d07df  -- the commit this fork branched
#                    from and still its master, so the comparison isolates the fork's changes
#   upstream binary  6a6e18f23d19cc6d8a738b4a1245c4ac86fd6dd77ea71b0b548bcb9c6fad5f55
#   param            9197d394cb3907c83ac3c021af7eef9241952030d2647af2fb045eab8d308853
#   compiler         g++ (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1) 13.3.0; each build from its OWN recipe
#   host             pi -- i9-13900K, 24 physical cores, Ubuntu, idle
#   pinning          OMP_PROC_BIND=true OMP_PLACES=cores OMP_MAX_ACTIVE_LEVELS=1,
#                    taskset -c 0-(threads-1), identical for both builds
#   order            3 repeats, the two builds INTERLEAVED at every cell
#
# ---- MEDIANS OF THREE, main loop time over a fixed 4-iteration budget ----
#
#   dE4 (m=7401, sparse under both policies)   1t      2t      4t      8t     16t     24t   1->24
#     upstream 6eaad8d                      49.20   49.11   47.79   47.12   46.85   46.92   1.05x
#     this fork (main)                      46.55   47.01   24.27   14.09    8.29    6.42   7.25x
#     fork advantage                         1.06x   1.04x   1.97x   3.34x   5.65x   7.31x
#
#   dE3 (m=6067, sparse since the promotion)   1t      2t      4t      8t     16t     24t   1->24
#     upstream 6eaad8d                     434.62  408.92  211.28  119.96   71.08   60.80   7.15x
#     this fork (main)                      31.33   31.63   16.40    9.63    5.69    4.57   6.86x
#     fork advantage                        13.87x  12.93x  12.88x  12.45x  12.50x  13.31x
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND IT IS THE dE3 ROW. Against the superseded table the fork's dE3 advantage goes
# from 2.67x to about 13x -- because the fork now takes the sparse route by default and upstream
# cannot thread that path at all.
#
# THE MOST INFORMATIVE NUMBER IS AT ONE THREAD: 434.62 s against 31.33 s, about 13.9x, BEFORE any
# threading enters. That gap is the route, not the parallelism, and the old table could not show it
# because the fork was defaulting to the same dense route upstream takes.
#
# AND A HONEST COUNTERWEIGHT: the fork's own 1->24 SCALING on dE3 FALLS, 7.73x to 6.86x. Nothing
# regressed -- the sparse route is simply so much faster at one thread that there is less left for
# threads to recover. Absolute time improved by ~5x at every thread count; the scaling RATIO got
# worse. Quoting the ratio alone would misrepresent both directions.
#
# ---- PRECISION ----
#
#   cell                     spread over 3 repeats
#   dE4 upstream 1t / 24t          0.0% / 0.1%
#   dE4 fork     1t / 24t          0.0% / 7.4%
#   dE3 upstream 1t / 24t          0.1% / 0.6%
#   dE3 fork     1t / 24t          0.1% / 6.7%
#
# The fork's 24-thread cells are short (4-6 s) and jittery at 6.7-7.4%; every other cell is
# essentially exact. So the 24-thread ADVANTAGE figures are good to about two significant figures
# -- "about 7.3x" and "about 13x" -- while the 1-thread ones are solid. The published tables quote
# accordingly.
#
# DETERMINISM: one objective per problem across all 18 fork runs, at every thread count.
